d for All Terminals or something. But I don't have enough yet.\n\nWait, my hand has T1 (1), T9 (2), W9 (1). Terminals are 1 or 9 of any suit. If I can get more terminals, maybe All Terminals. But All Terminals is 8 points. Let's see: I have T1, T9 (two), W9. If I can get B1, B9, etc., but I don't have those. So maybe not the best path.\n\nWhat about the Seven Pairs? Let me count: W2 (2), T9 (2), and if I can make F1, F2, F3 each a pair by getting another one. But I only have one of each. So unless I can draw another. But I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe discard some non-pair tiles to focus on pairs.\n\nAlternatively, maybe forming a Pure Straight or Mixed Straight. But with the current tiles, I have W2, W4, W7, W8, W9; T1, T4, T7, T9. Not sure if that can form a straight. For example, W2, W3, W4 (but I don't have W3), so maybe not.\n\nAnother thought: since I have F1, F2, F3 each once, maybe if I can get a fourth wind, I can form a Little Four Winds. But I need three pungs and a pair. Since I have one each of F1, F2, F3, I need to get another of one of them to make a pair. But I don't have another. So unless I draw another F1, F2, or F3. But I'm not sure.\n\nLooking at the valid actions again. The possible plays are Play F2, T1, W8, etc. Let me check each:\n\n- Play F2: I have one F2 in hand. Discarding it might help others, but I need to think if keeping it is better. Since I have F1, F2, F3, maybe keeping F2 is part of a strategy.\n\n- Play T1: Discarding a terminal tile. If I keep it, maybe for All Terminals, but not sure.\n\n- Play W8: I have one W8. If I discard, maybe someone else can use it. But if I keep, maybe for a Chow or Pung.\n\n- Play F3: I have one F3. Discarding it would leave me with F1 and F2. Maybe not helpful.\n\n- Play W9: I have one W9. Discarding it might be okay, but it's a terminal.\n\n- Play W7: I have one W7. Discarding it could be part of a Chow.\n\n- Play F1: I have one F1. Discarding it would leave me with F2 and F3.\n\n- Play T7: I have one T7. Discarding it might be part of a Chow.\n\n- Play W2: I have two W2s. Discarding one could help form a Pung if I draw another. But since I have two, maybe keeping them for a pair.\n\n- Play T9: I have two T9s. Discarding one would leave me with one, but maybe needed for a pair.\n\n- Play W4: I have one W4. Discarding it could be part of a Chow.\n\n- Play T4: I have one T4. Discarding it could be part of a Chow.\n\nHmm. Since I'm trying to build towards a Seven Pairs, maybe I should discard non-pair tiles. For example, if I discard W4, T4, W7, W8, etc., to focus on the pairs. Let's see: if I discard W4 and T4, that might help. But the valid actions include Play T4, so maybe I can play T4.\n\nWait, the valid actions are the ones I can discard. The idea is to choose the best tile to discard to optimize my hand. So which tile's discard would help me the most in forming a winning hand?\n\nIf I discard T4, which is a single tile, maybe that's better than keeping it. Because if I keep it, it's not part of a pair or a meld. Alternatively, if I discard W4, same thing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should focus on forming a Seven Pairs. I have two W2 and two T9. If I can get four more pairs, that would be seven. For example, if I can get F1 paired, F2 paired, F3 paired, etc. But I need to get another of each. So maybe discarding tiles that are not part of pairs would help. For example, if I discard W4, T4, W7, W8, etc.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the possible plays are Play F2, T1, W8, etc. Let me think: if I discard T1, that's a terminal, which might be useful for All Terminals, but I don't have enough. If I discard F2, which is part of the F1-F2-F3 sequence, but I'm not sure. Maybe keeping F2 is better if I can form a Little Four Winds. But I need three pungs and a pair. Since I have one of each, I need to get another of one to make a pair. But I don't have another. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should look for All Chows. But my current tiles don't seem to form many chows. For example, W2, W4, W7-9, T1, T4, T7-9. Not sure.\n\nAnother angle: the game history. The previous player has a Chi B6, which might mean they have B5, B6, B7. The player across has a Chi W7, so they might have W6, W7, W8. The next player has a Peng F4, so they have three F4s. Knowing this, maybe I can avoid discarding tiles that others might need. For example, if I discard W7, the player across might have W6 and W8, so they might need W7 for a Chi. But I already have W7 once. If I discard it, they might take it. But if I keep it, maybe I can use it in a Chi if I get another W6 or W8. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T1, which is a terminal, others might not need it for chows. So it's safer to discard.\n\nGiven that, maybe discarding T1 is a good move. It's a terminal, but if I don't need it for a specific pattern, getting rid of it might help. Also, since I have T9 (two copies), maybe focusing on terminals is better. But I need more.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. For example, W4 is a single tile. If I discard W4, that's a middle tile, which might not be needed for chows. Similarly, T4 is a middle tile. Discarding either could be good.\n\nLooking at the valid actions, the options include Play T4. If I play T4, which is a single tile, maybe that's better than keeping it. Let's see: my hand has T4 once. If I discard it, then I have T1, T7, T9. Maybe that's better for forming a terminal-based hand.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W4, which is a single tile. Both options are similar.\n\nBut I also have W2 twice. Keeping W2 as a pair is good. If I can get more pairs, that's better for Seven Pairs.\n\nSo, between discarding T4 or W4, which is better? If I discard T4, I lose a tile that could be part of a T1-T2-T3 chow, but I don't have T2 or T3. So maybe not. If I discard W4, same thing. Neither seems to help with chows. So either is acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard a