t for a pung. But since there's only one left, and I have a chow, maybe not.\n- T7: 1 left. The player across has one in their chow, so playing it might not help them.\n- B9: 1 left. Not part of any visible chows.\n- W2: 1 left. Same as W7.\n\nBetween these, B9 seems the safest because it's a terminal and not part of any visible chows. Playing B9 might not help others complete their hands. \n\nAlternatively, playing T4. The unshown T4 has 2 left. The previous player has a T4 in their meld [T4, T5, T6]. If I play T4, they already have one, but there's another left. They might need it for a pung. But since there's only one left, they can't. So playing T4 is safe. \n\nBut I have T3 and T4. If I keep T4, maybe I can form a chow with T3 and T5. But I don't have T5. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best move is to play B9. It's a terminal, not part of any visible chows, and unshown count is 1. Playing it would be the safest option.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to play B9 as it is a terminal tile not involved in any visible chows and has only one remaining in the unshown tiles, minimizing the risk of aiding opponents' melds.\nReason: B9 is a terminal tile with no visible chow dependencies. Discarding it avoids giving opponents tiles they might need for pungs or chows, especially since only one remains unshown. This maintains flexibility in your hand for forming additional chows or pairs while reducing immediate risk.\nAnswer: Play B9"}
